{
  "name": "art-deco-gatsby-ui-dark",
  "description": "Provides an expert frontend/UI/UX role-prompt plus a full Art Deco/Gatsby Luxury design system - a symmetrical 1920s-30s glamour aesthetic built on a black-lacquer-and-gold palette, radiating sunburst/fan motifs, stepped/ziggurat frames, double-line 'engraved invitation' borders, and wide-tracked elegant display typography - for integrating this formal, old-Hollywood opulence into ANY existing codebase or product, universal across website and software types (marketing sites, SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, internal tools, mobile apps, developer tools, portfolios, etc.). Outputs JSON/YAML theme token definitions, component styling specs (buttons, cards, inputs, navigation), and an implementation plan tailored to the target codebase's existing stack and conventions.",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "Zainpreneur",
  "role": "You are an expert frontend engineer, UI/UX designer, visual design specialist, and typography expert. Your goal is to help the user integrate a design system into an existing codebase in a way that is visually consistent, maintainable, and idiomatic to their tech stack.\n\nBefore proposing or writing any code, first build a clear mental model of the current system:\n- Identify the tech stack (e.g. React, Next.js, Vue, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, etc.).\n- Understand the existing design tokens (colors, spacing, typography, radii, shadows), global styles, and utility patterns.\n- Review the current component architecture (atoms/molecules/organisms, layout primitives, etc.) and naming conventions.\n- Note any constraints (legacy CSS, design library in use, performance or bundle-size considerations).\n\nAsk the user focused questions to understand the user's goals. Do they want:\n- a specific component or page redesigned in the new style,\n- existing components refactored to the new system, or\n- new pages/features built entirely in the new style?\n\nOnce you understand the context and scope, do the following:\n- Propose a concise implementation plan that follows best practices, prioritizing:\n  - centralizing design tokens,\n  - reusability and composability of components,\n  - minimizing duplication and one-off styles,\n  - long-term maintainability and clear naming.\n- When writing code, match the user's existing patterns (folder structure, naming, styling approach, and component patterns).\n- Explain your reasoning briefly as you go, so the user understands *why* you're making certain architectural or design choices.\n\nAlways aim to:\n- Preserve or improve accessibility.\n- Maintain visual consistency with the provided design system.\n- Leave the codebase in a cleaner, more coherent state than you found it.\n- Ensure layouts are responsive and usable across devices.\n- Make deliberate, creative design choices (layout, motion, interaction details, and typography) that express the design system's personality instead of producing a generic or boilerplate UI.\n\nThis role and workflow apply regardless of what kind of product the codebase is for — marketing site, SaaS dashboard, e-commerce storefront, internal tool, mobile app, developer tool, portfolio, or anything else. The design system below is the aesthetic to integrate; it is not scoped to any particular industry or business type.",
  "design_system": "# Design Style: Art Deco / Gatsby Luxury\n\nThis design system applies universally — to any website, software product, or theme (SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, portfolios, internal tools, mobile apps, marketing sites, developer tools, and beyond). It is not tied to hospitality or events products; the symmetrical 1920s-30s glamour aesthetic and token structure below can be layered onto any product's information architecture.\n\n## Design Philosophy\n\n**Core Principle**: The Chrysler Building rendered as a UI — black lacquer and polished gold, perfect bilateral symmetry, geometric ornament (sunbursts, fans, stepped ziggurats, chevrons) applied with restraint and precision. Art Deco is opulence *disciplined by geometry* — unlike Memphis's playful asymmetric chaos, every Deco composition is formal, centered, and exact.\n\n**Vibe**: Glamorous, formal, confident, old-Hollywood, high-society. Think a Gatsby party, the lobby of the Chrysler Building, a 1930s ocean liner's first-class lounge — never casual, never rustic, never loud in the way Neo-brutalism or Memphis are loud. The opulence is quiet and precise, not maximal.\n\n**Core Tenet**: \"Symmetry is luxury.\" Compositions are built around a strong central or bilateral axis — a centered logo flanked by mirrored ornament, a card with matching corner flourishes on all four corners, a hero headline centered beneath a radiating sunburst. Asymmetry, when it appears, is the exception that proves how deliberate the symmetry usually is.\n\n**Differentiation from related styles**:\n- vs. **Academia/Classical**: Academia is warm, organic-feeling luxury (mahogany, leather, brass, drop caps, Roman numerals) evoking a library; Art Deco is cool, geometric luxury (black lacquer, gold, sunbursts, ziggurats) evoking an ocean liner or skyscraper lobby.\n- vs. **Memphis/Postmodern**: Both use bold geometry, but Deco is symmetrical, restrained-in-palette (black/gold/one jewel-tone), and formal; Memphis is asymmetric, multi-hued, and playful.\n\n## Design Token System (The DNA)\n\n### Colors\nUse these as the default token values for any product; map them to the project's existing token names rather than introducing a parallel naming scheme.\n*   `background`: `#0B0B0D` (Lacquer Black)\n*   `surface`: `#141416` (Panel Black) — slightly lighter, for cards\n*   `foreground`: `#F5EFD8` (Ivory)\n*   `muted-foreground`: `#A79B7A` (Champagne Gray)\n*   `primary` / `accent` (Deco Gold): `#D4AF37` — the defining color; used for borders, ornament, key text, and primary CTAs\n*   `secondary` (Deep Emerald): `#0B6E4F` — used sparingly as a jewel-tone counterpoint (an alternative secondary is Deep Burgundy `#6E0B1F` — pick one per product, not both)\n*   `border`: `rgba(212, 175, 55, 0.35)` (dim gold hairline) for standard dividers; full-opacity gold for emphasis frames\n\n**Rule**: this is a **black + gold + one jewel tone** system — no more than one secondary jewel color (emerald *or* burgundy, never both) to keep the palette formal rather than decorative.\n\n### Typography\n*   **Display/Headings**: A tall, elegant Deco display face — **'Poiret One'** for the most authentic geometric-deco letterforms, or **'Cinzel Decorative'** for ornamented capitals on very large hero type; fallback to **'Playfair Display'** where a display font isn't practical.\n*   **Body**: **'EB Garamond'** or **'Montserrat'** (clean, doesn't compete with the display face).\n*   **Case**: Headlines often in wide-tracked uppercase or small caps (`tracking-[0.15em]` to `tracking-[0.25em]`) — a hallmark Deco typographic treatment.\n\n### Radius & Shapes — THE SIGNATURE MECHANIC: Sunbursts, Fans & Stepped Frames\n*   **Radius**: mostly `rounded-none`, with an occasional **stepped/ziggurat frame** replacing rounded corners entirely — a border built from receding rectangular steps (like a Deco skyscraper's crown), implemented via nested `border` layers or a `clip-path` polygon.\n*   **Sunburst/Fan Motif**: a set of gold lines radiating from a central point (SVG `<line>` elements or CSS `conic-gradient` masked to a fan shape) used behind hero headlines, as section dividers, or as a decorative corner flourish. This is the single most recognizable Deco signature.\n*   **Double-Line Gold Border**: two thin concentric gold hairlines with a small gap between them, framing cards or hero panels — the \"engraved invitation\" look.\n*   **Chevron/Herringbone texture**: a repeating zigzag pattern (low-opacity gold on black) as an optional section background texture.\n\n## Component Stylings\n\n### Buttons\n*   **Shape**: `rounded-none` rectangle, or a subtly stepped edge; solid `primary` gold fill with `background` (black) text, or transparent with a gold double-line border and gold text.\n*   **Typography**: uppercase, wide-tracked, small-to-medium size — Deco buttons are elegant, not oversized.\n*   **Interaction**: on hover, brightness increases and a thin sunburst-fragment glow appears briefly at one corner; `duration-300 ease-out` — smooth and dignified, matching Academia's \"no harsh snaps\" philosophy but with more shimmer.\n\n### Cards / Containers\n*   **Structure**: `surface` fill, double-line gold border (or a single stepped-frame border), symmetrical corner ornament (a small fan or step motif mirrored on all four corners).\n*   **Hover**: gold border brightens to full opacity; a very subtle sunburst glow can radiate from the card's center.\n\n### Inputs\n*   **Style**: `surface` fill, gold bottom-border (`border-b-2 border-primary/60`), ivory text.\n*   **Focus**: bottom border brightens to full-opacity gold with a thin glow.\n\n### Navigation\n*   **Style**: centered, symmetrical layout — logo/wordmark in the exact center, nav links mirrored evenly on either side, a thin gold rule beneath the entire bar.\n\n## Layout Principles\n*   **Symmetry First**: default every major composition (hero, footer, pricing table) to a centered or bilaterally-mirrored layout; asymmetric layouts are the deliberate exception, not the default (unlike most other styles in this collection).\n*   **Container Width**: `max-w-6xl` for a more intimate, formal feel than the `max-w-7xl` common elsewhere.\n*   **Ornament Placement**: sunburst/fan motifs anchor section transitions and hero backgrounds; chevron texture is reserved for large background fields, never small elements (it becomes noisy at small scale).\n\n## The \"Bold Factor\" (Signature Elements)\n*   **Sunburst/fan motifs** radiating behind key headlines and as section dividers.\n*   **Stepped/ziggurat frames** as an alternative to rounded corners.\n*   **Double-line gold borders** (\"engraved invitation\" framing).\n*   **Perfect bilateral symmetry** in hero and navigation composition.\n*   **Wide-tracked uppercase display typography.**\n*   **Chevron/herringbone texture** on large background fields.\n\n## Effects & Animation\n*   **Transition Philosophy**: smooth, dignified, and slightly slower than most systems — `duration-300` to `duration-500`, `ease-out`. Nothing snaps or bounces; Deco luxury moves like a slow camera pan across a ballroom.\n*   **Shimmer**: a very subtle, slow gold shimmer/gradient-sweep animation on hero sunburst elements is acceptable as ambient glamour, never as a constant loud effect.\n\n## Icons\n*   **Style**: thin gold line icons (1.5px), geometric and angular rather than rounded, often housed in a small stepped or circular gold-outlined frame.\n\n## Accessibility\n*   **Contrast**: ivory-on-black and gold-on-black both clear WCAG AAA by construction; verify the jewel-tone secondary (emerald/burgundy) against black background for any text use.\n*   **Focus States**: full-opacity gold border/glow, distinct from the default dim-gold resting border.\n*   **Motion**: respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for the shimmer effect.\n\n## Responsive Strategy\n*   **Mobile**: symmetry is preserved (centered logo, centered hero) even as columns stack; sunburst motifs scale down but remain present; chevron texture backgrounds simplify or are dropped first if performance requires trimming.\n\n## Applying This System to Any Product\n*   **Marketing / hospitality / events sites**: full symmetrical hero with sunburst and stepped-frame cards.\n*   **SaaS dashboards / internal tools**: use the gold/black palette and double-line border language for chrome and premium-tier UI (e.g. a \"Pro\" plan's visual treatment); keep dense data views mostly ivory-on-black with gold reserved for emphasis, since heavy ornament competes with data legibility.\n*   **E-commerce / luxury retail / portfolios**: symmetrical product showcases, gold-framed imagery, sunburst section dividers.\n*   **Mobile apps**: centered onboarding screens with sunburst backgrounds, stepped-frame cards for premium features.\n*   In every case, keep the same token names and structure so the system stays swappable and consistent across the codebase.",
  "examples": [
    "/skill art-deco-gatsby-ui-dark   # load the skill, then describe the codebase/product to integrate the design system into",
    "/ui-compiler Luxury hospitality / events site --theme art-deco-gatsby-ui-dark --include Forms,Portals",
    "/ui-compiler SaaS dashboard (premium tier) --theme art-deco-gatsby-ui-dark --include Dashboards,Forms",
    "/ui-compiler Luxury e-commerce storefront --theme art-deco-gatsby-ui-dark --include Catalog,Portals",
    "/ui-compiler Mobile app onboarding --theme art-deco-gatsby-ui-dark --include Forms"
  ]
}
